Addresses three critical issues in the multi-device authentication implementation that would lock out existing users and crash new user settings updates.

## Legacy User Migration

Pre-existing users without `auth_password`/`auth_password_iv` (created before the canonical password rollout) now bootstrap a secure password on first login instead of receiving a 500 error:

```typescript
// Generate and store canonical password for legacy users
const legacyCanonicalPassword = crypto.randomBytes(32).toString("base64");
const { content: encryptedData, iv } = encrypt(legacyCanonicalPassword);

// Conditional update prevents race conditions
const { data: updateResult } = await supabaseAdmin
  .from("users")
  .update({ auth_password: encryptedData, auth_password_iv: iv })
  .eq("id", verifieduserId)
  .is("auth_password", null)  // Only update if not already set
  .select("auth_password, auth_password_iv");

// If another request won the race, refetch and decrypt the stored password
if (!updateResult || updateResult.length === 0) {
  const { data: refetchedData } = await supabaseAdmin
    .from("users")
    .select("auth_password, auth_password_iv")
    .eq("id", verifieduserId)
    .single();
  passwordToUse = decrypt(refetchedData.auth_password_iv, refetchedData.auth_password);
}
```

## Null Safety in Optimistic Updates

Fixed runtime crash when new users (with `null` settings) perform their first settings mutation:

```typescript
// Before: Crashes on null spread
const optimisticData = { ...previousSettings, ...newSettings };

// After: Safely defaults to empty object
const optimisticData = { ...(previousSettings || {}), ...newSettings };
```

